Ladybird commits computed animation values through the animation’s
position in the effect stack, including its effect after replacement.
It samples a private style working set with endpoint-inclusive timing,
then writes the targeted physical longhands and custom properties into
one inline-style update. The sample leaves live timing, effect lists,
and animated style overlays unchanged.

Blink’s Animation::commitStyles uses ScopedCommitStylesTiming and
KeyframeEffect::InterpolationsForCommitStyles to obtain a partial stack
and restore its normal timing afterward. Gecko’s Animation::CommitStyles
uses EffectCompositor::ComposeServoAnimationRuleForEffect to compose
through the committing effect, including it outside the relevant set.
WebKit’s WebAnimation::commitStyles resolves ordered effects through the
committing animation with EndpointInclusiveActiveInterval::Yes.
Ladybird uses its own keyframe-description and sampling interfaces for
the same operation, without temporarily changing the rendering stack.

Ladybird writes computed values rather than resolved layout values:
relative lengths are computed, numeric line-height remains numeric,
and transforms retain their computed transform-list representation.
WebKit’s WebAnimation::commitStyles selects the computed property value
in Style::Extractor. Blink serializes the computed values through
AnimationUtils::ForEachInterpolatedPropertyValue, and Gecko uses
Servo_DeclarationBlock_SetPropertyToAnimationValue. All three avoid
redundant declaration writes, as Ladybird’s batch setter does.

Ladybird follows the specification for display:contents targets and
animations without a target. Gecko’s Animation::CommitStyles returns for
an absent effect or target, and Element::IsRendered accepts an element
with display:contents. Blink’s Animation::commitStyles and WebKit’s
WebAnimation::commitStyles require a styled target and a layout object
or renderer. Chrome 157 throws for those two cases. Ladybird follows
the specification, including its explicit display:contents rule.

Ladybird retains the transform interpolator’s primitive forms when
committing them, so translateX can become translate, and matrix can
become matrix3d. Chrome retains more of the original function names
and omits redundant arguments. These serialization differences leave
the rendered transforms unchanged after committing and canceling.

Ladybird commits zoomed lengths in unscaled CSS units. A registered
custom property uses its computed initial value when neither the
partial effect stack nor the base custom-property data supplies a
value. Committing styles flushes pending style changes, and its
layout reason is classified as requiring computed style.

Blink serializes committed values through AnimationUtils::
ForEachInterpolatedPropertyValue and computed-style property
conversion. ComputedStyleUtils::ZoomAdjustedPixelValueForLength
undoes zoom, and CustomProperty::CSSValueFromComputedStyleInternal
reads the registered value from the computed style. Chrome 157
retains a 100px inline width at effective zooms 2, 3, and 0.5, and
commits a registered 1in initial value as 96px during a start delay.

WebKit resolves the partial effect stack in WebAnimation::
commitStyles, then uses Style::Extractor to serialize its computed
values. Its custom-property extraction reads the style seeded by
CustomPropertyRegistry::initialValuePrototypeStyle, which includes
registered initial values. These conversions produce CSS values
that can be applied as declarations without scaling them twice.

Gecko composes the partial stack through EffectCompositor::
ComposeServoAnimationRuleForEffect. CSSPixelLength::
to_animated_value removes effective zoom, and AnimationValue::
uncompute converts the sampled values into declarations. Its
custom-property animation values come from the computed style via
AnimationValue::from_computed_values. All three engines flush style
before committing, matching the pending-style control in this change.
